    "text": "To solve the problem, we start by understanding the relationship between \\(a\\) and \\(b^2\\). The problem states that \\(a\\) varies inversely with \\(b^2\\). This means that there exists a constant \\(k\\) such that:\n\n\\[ a = \\frac{k}{b^2} \\]\n\nWe are given that \\(a = 9\\) when \\(b = 2\\). We can use this information to find the constant \\(k\\). Substituting the given values into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[ 9 = \\frac{k}{2^2} \\]\n\nSince \\(2^2 = 4\\), the equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[ 9 = \\frac{k}{4} \\]\n\nTo solve for \\(k\\), we multiply both sides of the equation by 4:\n\n\\[ k = 9 \\times 4 = 36 \\]\n\nNow that we have the constant \\(k\\), we can use it to find the value of \\(a\\) when \\(b = 3\\). Substituting \\(b = 3\\) and \\(k = 36\\) into the equation \\(a = \\frac{k}{b^2}\\), we get:\n\n\\[ a = \\frac{36}{3^2} \\]\n\nSince \\(3^2 = 9\\), the equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[ a = \\frac{36}{9} = 4 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) when \\(b = 3\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
